  "question": {
    "text": "Assume there are two identical simple pendulum Clocks-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math> is placed on the earth and Clock-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> is placed on a space station located at a height h above the earth surface. Clock-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math> and Clock-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> operate at time periods <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math> respectively. Then the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>h</mi></math> is -<br />(consider radius of earth <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>6400</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>km</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi></math> on earth <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>)"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1200</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>km</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
